#!/bin/bash
# HPC Manager for LIP6 Cluster
# Allaa Boutaleb - allaa.boutaleb@lip6.fr
# No "set -e": a failed ssh/oarsub/oarstat must print why and return to the menu, never end the cockpit.

# ---- BEGIN shared helpers (keep identical in conv-manager and hpc-notebook) ----
# Runs on macOS (bash 3.2), Linux, WSL and Git Bash: no python, no GNU-only flags.

# Colors only on a terminal, and never with NO_COLOR set (https://no-color.org)
if [ -t 1 ] && [ -z "${NO_COLOR:-}" ]; then
    BLUE='\033[1;34m'
    GREEN='\033[1;32m'
    YELLOW='\033[1;33m'
    RED='\033[1;31m'
    CYAN='\033[1;36m'
    PURPLE='\033[1;35m'
    DIM='\033[2m'
    BOLD='\033[1m'
    NC='\033[0m'
else
    BLUE='' GREEN='' YELLOW='' RED='' CYAN='' PURPLE='' DIM='' BOLD='' NC=''
fi

case "$(uname -s 2>/dev/null)" in
    Darwin) PLATFORM=mac ;;
    Linux) if grep -qi microsoft /proc/version 2>/dev/null; then PLATFORM=wsl; else PLATFORM=linux; fi ;;
    MINGW*|MSYS*|CYGWIN*) PLATFORM=gitbash ;;
    *) PLATFORM=linux ;;
esac

# One shared ssh connection per cluster for 10 min, so each menu action skips the login handshake.
# Windows' ssh has no connection sharing; LIP6_NO_MUX=1 turns it off anywhere.
SSH_MUX=()
if [ "$PLATFORM" != gitbash ] && [ "${LIP6_NO_MUX:-}" != 1 ]; then
    SSH_MUX=(-o ControlMaster=auto -o "ControlPath=~/.ssh/cm-%C" -o ControlPersist=10m
        -o ServerAliveInterval=15 -o ServerAliveCountMax=3 -o ConnectTimeout=10)
fi
rssh() { ssh ${SSH_MUX[@]+"${SSH_MUX[@]}"} "$@"; }

# Open tunnels, one file per job: "pid local_port target" (and the tunnel's ssh output in <job>.log)
TUNNEL_DIR="${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/lip6-cluster/tunnels"
mkdir -p "$TUNNEL_DIR" 2>/dev/null
chmod 700 "$TUNNEL_DIR" 2>/dev/null
# Older versions kept them in /tmp: adopt those once (only from a real directory we own)
OLD_TUNNEL_DIR="/tmp/jupyter_tunnels_$(whoami 2>/dev/null)"
if [ -d "$OLD_TUNNEL_DIR" ] && [ ! -L "$OLD_TUNNEL_DIR" ] && [ -O "$OLD_TUNNEL_DIR" ]; then
    mv -f "$OLD_TUNNEL_DIR"/* "$TUNNEL_DIR"/ 2>/dev/null
    rmdir "$OLD_TUNNEL_DIR" 2>/dev/null
fi

# port_in_use PORT: something on this machine accepts connections on 127.0.0.1:PORT
port_in_use() { (exec 3<>"/dev/tcp/127.0.0.1/$1") 2>/dev/null; }

# find_free_port: a local port nothing listens on, from a random start in 20000-39999
find_free_port() {
    local port=$((20000 + RANDOM % 20000)) tries=0
    while [ $tries -lt 50 ]; do
        port_in_use "$port" || { echo "$port"; return 0; }
        port=$((port >= 39999 ? 20000 : port + 1))
        tries=$((tries + 1))
    done
    return 1
}

# open_url URL: show URL in the browser when this machine can; never fails (the caller prints the URL)
open_url() {
    case $PLATFORM in
        mac) open "$1" ;;
        wsl) command -v wslview >/dev/null 2>&1 && wslview "$1" \
            || { command -v explorer.exe >/dev/null 2>&1 && { explorer.exe "$1"; true; }; } ;;
        linux) [ -n "${DISPLAY:-}${WAYLAND_DISPLAY:-}" ] && command -v xdg-open >/dev/null 2>&1 && { xdg-open "$1" & } ;;
        gitbash) start "" "$1" || cmd.exe //c start "" "$1" ;;
    esac </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || echo -e "${DIM}No browser could be opened from here: copy the URL above into your browser.${NC}"
}

# tunnel_start JOBID TARGET SSH_ARGS...: forward a free local port to TARGET through `ssh SSH_ARGS` (the host last).
# The tunnel ignores hangups, so it outlives this window; a live one to the same place is reused.
# Sets TUNNEL_PORT; on failure says why and returns 1.
tunnel_start() {
    local jobid=$1 target=$2 log pid deadline old_pid old_port old_target
    shift 2
    read -r old_pid old_port old_target <<< "$(tunnel_list | awk -v j="$jobid" '$1 == j { print $2, $3, $4 }')"
    if [ -n "$old_port" ] && [ "$old_target" = "$target" ]; then
        TUNNEL_PORT=$old_port
        return 0
    fi
    [ -z "$old_pid" ] || kill "$old_pid" 2>/dev/null
    TUNNEL_PORT=$(find_free_port) || { echo -e "${RED}No free local port found.${NC}"; return 1; }
    log="$TUNNEL_DIR/$jobid.log"
    ( trap '' HUP; exec ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -o ControlMaster=no -o ControlPath=none \
        -o ServerAliveInterval=30 -L "127.0.0.1:$TUNNEL_PORT:$target" "$@" ) </dev/null >"$log" 2>&1 &
    pid=$!
    deadline=$((SECONDS + 20))
    while kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null && [ $SECONDS -lt $deadline ]; do
        if port_in_use "$TUNNEL_PORT"; then
            echo "$pid $TUNNEL_PORT $target" > "$TUNNEL_DIR/$jobid"
            return 0
        fi
        sleep 0.2
    done
    kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null
    echo -e "${RED}Tunnel failed to start.${NC}"
    tail -n 5 "$log" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/    /'
    return 1
}

# tunnel_list: "jobid pid port target" for each live tunnel (process up and port answering); forgets dead ones
tunnel_list() {
    local f pid port target
    for f in "$TUNNEL_DIR"/*; do
        case $f in *.log) continue ;; esac
        [ -f "$f" ] || continue
        read -r pid port target < "$f"
        if [ -n "$port" ] && kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null && port_in_use "$port"; then
            echo "${f##*/} $pid $port ${target:-?}"
        else
            rm -f "$f" "$f.log"
        fi
    done
}

# sever_tunnel: list the live tunnels and close the one the user names, or all of them
sever_tunnel() {
    local list jid pid port target choice hit=false
    echo ""
    list=$(tunnel_list)
    if [ -z "$list" ]; then
        echo -e "${DIM}No active tunnels.${NC}"
        return
    fi
    while read -r jid pid port target; do
        echo -e "  ${GREEN}Job $jid${NC} — localhost:$port → $target (PID $pid)"
    done <<< "$list"
    echo ""
    echo -e "Enter a job ID to disconnect, or ${BOLD}all${NC} to disconnect everything."
    ask "> " choice
    [ -n "$choice" ] || return
    while read -r jid pid port target; do
        if [ "$choice" = all ] || [ "$choice" = "$jid" ]; then
            kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null
            rm -f "$TUNNEL_DIR/$jid" "$TUNNEL_DIR/$jid.log"
            echo -e "${GREEN}Tunnel for job $jid disconnected.${NC}"
            hit=true
        fi
    done <<< "$list"
    $hit || echo -e "${RED}Invalid choice.${NC}"
}
# ---- END shared helpers ----

# Job mail recipient; lip6-cluster-setup fills this in (empty = no mail)
MAIL_TO=""

# stderr of the last ssh call lands here, so a failure can say why
SSH_ERR="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/lip6-ssh-err.$$"
trap 'rm -f "$SSH_ERR"' EXIT

# ask PROMPT VAR: read one line; end of input (Ctrl-D, closed stdin) quits instead of spinning
ask() { read -r -p "$1" "$2" || { echo -e "\n${DIM}Bye!${NC}\n"; exit 0; }; }

# ssh_failed WHAT: say what failed, the ssh/OAR message saved in $SSH_ERR, and what to try next
ssh_failed() {
    echo -e "${RED}$1${NC}"
    [ -s "$SSH_ERR" ] && tail -n 5 "$SSH_ERR" | sed 's/^/    /'
    echo -e "${DIM}Check the message above, your network/VPN and the job id, then try again (test the link: ssh hpc true).${NC}"
}

# ssh_session ARGS: interactive ssh; its status is the remote shell's, only 255 means ssh itself failed
ssh_session() {
    rssh "$@"
    [ $? -ne 255 ] || echo -e "\n${RED}Could not connect.${NC} ${DIM}Check your network/VPN, then try again (test the link: ssh hpc true).${NC}"
}

# submit_failed OUT: oarsub gave no job id; show its message (or the ssh error) and the next step
submit_failed() {
    echo -e "${RED}Job was NOT submitted.${NC}"
    if [ -n "$1" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$1" | tail -n 8 | sed 's/^/    /'
    elif [ -s "$SSH_ERR" ]; then
        tail -n 5 "$SSH_ERR" | sed 's/^/    /'
    fi
    echo -e "${DIM}Fix the cause above (e.g. ask for fewer cores or less time) and try again. If unsure whether it went through, check Jobs (option 3) first.${NC}"
}

STATUS_LOGIN="not checked"
